**@fabpot, on your question about prefixing the rate limiter with the transport name:** no prefix, and the branch already had it right. It resolves `rate_limiter: foo_limiter` to the `limiter.foo_limiter` service, which is exactly what Messenger does for a transport's rate limiter. Same line, same shape:

```php
// FrameworkExtension, messenger transports (existing)
$transportRateLimiterReferences[$name] = new Reference('limiter.'.$transport['rate_limiter']);
// FrameworkExtension, mailer transports (this PR)
$transportRateLimiterReferences[$name] = new Reference('limiter.'.$transport['rate_limiter']);
```

`limiter.` is the service id every `framework.rate_limiter.*` entry is registered under, not a new namespace. Prefixing with the transport name would also stop a limiter being shared, which is what you want when a provider's cap applies to the whole account rather than one transport.

