by nicolas-grekas at 2026-08-15T07:59:13Z

@fritzmg heads up on a behaviour change I pushed, please confirm you are happy with it before this gets merged.

The transport consumed the limiter and failed when it was over budget:

```php
$rateLimiter?->consume(1)->ensureAccepted();   // throws RateLimitExceededException
```

It now paces instead:

```php
$rateLimiter?->reserve()->wait();
```

My reasoning is that both precedents wait rather than throw:

* `Worker::rateLimit()` in Messenger consumes, and when the token is refused it dispatches `WorkerRateLimitedEvent` and calls `$rateLimiter->reserve()->wait()`;
* `AbstractTransport` itself, the very class this touches, already throttles by sleeping in `setMaxPerSecond()`: `$sleep = (1 / $this->rate) - (microtime(true) - $this->lastSent); usleep(...)`.

For the case in your description, a host that allows 500 mails an hour, throwing means the 501st message fails rather than being sent a little later, and a synchronous send loses it unless the caller catches the exception. "Rate limited transport" reads as pacing to me.

The trade is that a send can now block. That is already true of `setMaxPerSecond()`, and the option is opt-in, but it is a real difference: with a low limit and a synchronous send you can sit in a long `usleep`. If you would rather keep failing fast, or want `reserve(1, $maxTime)` so it waits up to a bound and throws beyond it, say so and I will change it back. No test asserted either behaviour, so nothing forced the choice.

Also in this push: `setRateLimiterFactory()` gained the summary line and `@return $this` that its neighbour `setMaxPerSecond()` carries, and the config option's `info()` now says it paces the messages rather than "to use when sending".

